  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CDWPCC3G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**no concurrency — one active graph at a time, explicit start/restart only**",
    "text": "**no concurrency — one active graph at a time, explicit start/restart only**: Clarified a confusion in the write-up above: a service holding a graph that's stable for its whole lifetime *was* the intent all along — the actual bug was that any command could *silently* auto-spawn one, so whichever command happened to run first invisibly decided the binding for as long as that process lived. The fix isn't to thread cwd through every request (that would mean re-resolving, i.e. potentially re-binding, on every call — not stable, and not the intent); it's to make the one moment of binding explicit and human-driven instead of accidental.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ5ET5Z8003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CDX5H5MG002",
      "BlockNode:00CDX5H5MG003",
      "BlockNode:00CDX5H5MG004",
      "BlockNode:00CDX5H5MG005"
    ]
  },
