    ],
    "title": "**Stale `TreeView.unfolds` references have no general cleanup**",
    "text": "**Stale `TreeView.unfolds` references have no general cleanup**: `TreeView.unfold(ref)` appended with no existence check, and the only cleanup — a reactive strip-on-hard-delete — only fired at the exact moment a specific id was hard-deleted; nothing revisited an *existing* `unfolds` entry against what's currently live. Confirmed live: `.state/TreeView.jsonld`'s `default` view had named 10 `BlockNode` ids with no corresponding document at all, after unrelated reconciliation churn elsewhere reassigned/removed the blocks those ids used to name. See [Settled](../discussion/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1FFSR8006) for the chosen fix direction. **Resolved**: `TreeView.unfold(ref)` now refuses (silent no-op) a ref with zero quads at write time, and a new `pruneStaleUnfolds` sweep runs at the same explicit-sweep points as `pruneUnreachableTombstones` to catch a ref going stale later. The corpus's own `default` view was already clean of stale ids by the time the sweep landed (0 pruned on a real run) — see [Settled](../discussion/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1FFSR8006) for the mechanism and its `verify.ts` coverage.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1GW63800A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1GW638005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A bare `aperas unfold <ref>` (no `--view` flag at all) mutates the `\"default\"` view instead of peeking**",
    "text": "**A bare `aperas unfold <ref>` (no `--view` flag at all) mutates the `\"default\"` view instead of peeking**: should be read-only — resolve and print a preview, touching no `TreeView` state — matching `aperas tree`'s own no-view default. It fell into the same bootstrap-and-mutate path that `aperas unfold <ref> --view` (flag present, no name) is supposed to trigger. `aperas fold`'s own no-`--view` case isn't resolved by this — folding presupposes some view's state to collapse, so a peek-equivalent doesn't obviously apply. **Resolved**: `kgUnfold.ts` now distinguishes the `--view` flag's outright absence (peek — `runUnfold` given `view: null`, no `resolveTreeView`/`ensureDefaultView` call at all) from the flag present with no name following it (unchanged bootstrap-and-mutate default-view behavior). Verified live: a bare `aperas unfold` on an already-unfolded ref left the default view's `unfolds` count unchanged (43 -> 43), while `--view default` on a fresh ref still grew it (43 -> 44).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1GW63800A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1GW638006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1GW638006:links:Link:00CGRWJ5XG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CEDP10P8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1GW638006:links:Link:00CGRWJ5XG000:props:StringProp:00CGRWJ5XG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "733"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**No raw single-node inspection command**",
    "text": "**No raw single-node inspection command**: `aperas tree`/`aperas backlinks --text` only ever show a rendered preview (title + truncated, anchor-stripped abstract) — right for browsing, not for verifying a stored field is exactly what's about to be pushed back via `aperas update`/`aperas insert`, or for reading a field no command shows at all (`props`, `tombstonedAt`). `aperas project --dry-run` reconstructs real markdown but only at the whole-artifact level. Before this, the only way to see a node's exact stored state was a direct `BlockNode.jsonld`/`ArtifactNode.jsonld` read outside the CLI entirely. Originally tracked under packaging, relocated here since it's a node-inspection gap, not a packaging one — the same gap the [`aperas` skill's own item 8](../history/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CEDP10P8001) independently names — that gap's skill-script stage; `scripts/show_node.py` staged the gap, `aperas show <ref>` below was the still-open toolset-update stage of the same lifecycle. **Resolved**: `aperas show <ref>` (`kgShow.ts`) resolves the normal deep-path way, then prints the node's exact stored fields via `dehydrate.ts`'s own `serializeDoc` — the identical per-kind shape walk that writes the on-disk mirror, reused rather than re-implemented, so \"what `show` prints\" can't drift from \"what's actually on disk.\" `--text` prints only the raw `text` field, undecorated, meant to be redirected to a file and piped back into `kg:update` for a byte-identical round-trip.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1GW63800A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1GW638007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
