    "title": "**`aperas unfold`'s raw preview omits the tombstone tag `aperas tree --view` shows**",
    "text": "**`aperas unfold`'s raw preview omits the tombstone tag `aperas tree --view` shows**: found live while investigating a cross-linking question in `list-consumption` — `aperas unfold BlockNode:00CE1GW638002 --view <name>` printed two children (00CE5QD6W8001, 00CE1GW638003) with no marker distinguishing them from live siblings, read as stray/anomalous at first. Re-running `aperas tree BlockNode:00CE1GW638002 --view <name>` on the identical, already-unfolded subtree appended a plain `(tombstoned)` suffix to those same two lines — confirming both commands know the node is tombstoned, but only `tree` surfaces it. **Proposed fix — don't just add the tag to `unfold`; hide tombstoned nodes from default output entirely, in both commands**, revealed only via an explicit `--tombstoned` flag. A tombstoned node is dead data retained solely for id-stability; showing it unmarked in a raw exploratory listing invites exactly the misreading that happened here. **Built exactly as proposed**: `TreeOptions.showTombstoned` (default `false`) now gates both renderers `kg:tree` uses (the plain no-`--view` recursion and the view-based `emitNode`/`emitLinkLine`) and `kg:unfold`'s own separate preview path — a tombstoned node is skipped entirely (not just its line; nothing recurses into it) unless `--tombstoned` is passed, in which case it's shown tagged `(tombstoned)`, same wording as before. `kg:unfold` additionally refuses to unfold a tombstoned node *directly* without the flag (a clear error, not silent emptiness), since that command's whole purpose is revealing the one thing it was pointed at. See [Issues](../issues/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE8T6W7G001) for the tracked gap.",
