      }
    ],
    "title": "**A parent-heading list-push reconciles children by array position, tombstones included, not by content/key**",
    "text": "**A parent-heading list-push reconciles children by array position, tombstones included, not by content/key**: found running `aperas-skill`'s own eval against [issues/treeview.md's Open Issues list](../issues/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW638002) — piping the heading line plus every visible item (skipping tombstoned ones, as any reasonable read would) zips the piped items against storage slots by raw position, silently reassigning live content onto wrong ids once a tombstoned sibling is interleaved. Contradicts what both the skill and the code's own doc comments claim (\"exact-key matching\"). Root cause not yet read off `reconcile.ts` directly — identified from reproduced symptom, confirmed only in a disposable eval sandbox, not the live corpus. Same defect family as the next two items below — one fix in `reconcile.ts` likely closes all three. Root cause now read directly: leaf matching (`diffChildren`'s `matchKeyed`) runs `gestaltMatchingBlocks` — the same recursive longest-exact-run algorithm as `matchLeftoverByAbstract` (item 3 below), just keyed on exact string equality rather than similarity — over the *piped* array against the *live (non-tombstoned) child* array. It is genuinely exact-key matching, not raw positional zipping; the recursion's own left/right split around each matched run is what a skipped tombstoned sibling can throw off when duplicate or near-duplicate keys sit on either side of the gap. That root-cause note was wrong, corrected here rather than left standing next to the fix that contradicts it: `listItem` is a `CONTAINER_TYPE`, not a `LEAF_TYPE`, so it never reaches `matchKeyed`/Stage A at all. The real mechanism is Stage B's per-type container alignment, which zipped old/new by raw array position with zero content comparison — confirmed by direct reproduction (a 4-item list missing its already-tombstoned 2nd item: item 3's content landed on item 2's dead id, item 4's content landed on item 3's id, item 4's own id was tombstoned). **Resolved**: Stage B now keys `listItem` buckets through `matchKeyed`+`leafKey`, the same machinery Stage A's leaves already use, instead of a positional zip — verified against the reproduction above (each item now lands on its own real id) and against an ordinary reordering/insert-in-the-middle case (also previously mismatched, not only the tombstone case). Same fix as [item 2 below](#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG004).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG004:links:Link:00CGRWEK7R000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG004:links:Link:00CGRWEK7R000:props:StringProp:00CGRWEK7R001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "978"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`aperas update`'s reconciliation can silently match fresh content onto an already-tombstoned node's id without reviving it**",
    "text": "**`aperas update`'s reconciliation can silently match fresh content onto an already-tombstoned node's id without reviving it**: reproduced twice in `list-consumption`'s eval sandbox — a new bullet's content lands on a stale, already-tombstoned id instead of a fresh one, and `tombstonedAt` is never cleared, so the content stays invisible even though the summary line reports a clean match/add. **Resolved**: root cause was every old-side matching pool (Stage A's leaves and heading buckets, Stage B's containers) including tombstoned candidates with no exclusion at all — a live new node's content could bind to a dead id purely because its stripped text happened to match. Fixed by excluding every tombstoned old child from every matching pool in `diffChildren`: a dead slot is never reused, new content always gets a fresh id instead. Verified live: a new item with text identical to an already-tombstoned sibling's now lands on a brand-new id, not the dead one. Same fix as [item 1 above](#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG003).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG005:links:Link:00CGRWEKXR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG005:links:Link:00CGRWEKXR000:props:StringProp:00CGRWEKXR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "851"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`matchLeftoverByAbstract` (Gestalt/Ratcliff-Obershelp recursion) is position-sensitive**",
    "text": "**`matchLeftoverByAbstract` (Gestalt/Ratcliff-Obershelp recursion) is position-sensitive**: works for reconciling siblings that share a rough common order, not for an unordered bag of whole-artifact identities scattered across concern folders — a match's recursive split can discard a whole quadrant of otherwise-valid candidates. Worked around for the rename path specifically (replaced by exact-key matching there), but the same algorithm was used elsewhere in `reconcile.ts` and still carried the weakness on those other paths. **Resolved**: confirmed narrower in scope than first filed — `matchLeftoverByAbstract` is used only by `trackAllArtifacts`/`trackArtifactsScoped` (`artifacts.ts`) and folder rename detection (`folders.ts`), never reached by list-item matching (that path already went through the separately-fixed `matchKeyed`+`leafKey`, [item 1 above](#id/BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG003)). `trackArtifactsScoped` already had its own order-independent workaround (`matchByExactKey`), duplicating the real fix rather than sharing it. Rewrote `matchLeftoverByAbstract` itself to exact-key matching with no positional requirement at all, and pointed both remaining call sites (whole-corpus artifact/folder rename detection) at it, retiring the now-redundant local copy. Verified with the exact reproduction this issue was filed from — a scrambled 4-item removed/added pair in fully reversed order — matching all 4 correctly where the old recursive algorithm stranded 2 of them in an already-discarded quadrant.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A fully-dead container is never itself tombstoned**",
    "text": "**A fully-dead container is never itself tombstoned**: `pruneUnreachableTombstones` only collected nodes that were themselves tombstoned and unreachable; nothing ever tombstoned a container once its last live child was gone (children get tombstoned one at a time as they're resolved, but the now-vacuous wrapper was never itself marked dead). Confirmed live against `issues/packaging.md`'s own dead nested-list-wrapper chain. **Resolved**: added `tombstoneVacuousContainers`, a companion sweep run right before `pruneUnreachableTombstones` at all three of its own explicit-sweep call sites (`kg:reload`, `kg:flush --clobber`, service shutdown) — marks `tombstonedAt` on a `list` node with no live children, or a `listItem` carrying no `.text` of its own and no live children (a pure structural wrapper), repeating to a fixed point since tombstoning an inner wrapper can vacate its own parent in turn. Verified live: an `outer wrapper item` `listItem` (no text of its own) holding one nested item, with the nested item removed via `aperas remove`, correctly gained its own `tombstonedAt` on the next reload sweep.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A multi-artifact `kg:ingest` isn't transactional either**",
    "text": "**A multi-artifact `kg:ingest` isn't transactional either**: `ingestArtifacts`/`ingestAllArtifacts` (`apeironNgn/artifacts.ts`) loop per file with no try/catch, so a later file's failure leaves earlier files' writes already committed to the in-memory store with no rollback across the batch. Same shape as the single-`kg:insert` gap above, one layer up (a batch of artifacts vs. a batch of nodes within one call). **Resolved**, but not with that fix: true all-or-nothing would need a store-wide snapshot/rollback primitive that doesn't exist here, a bigger undertaking than this item's scope. Chosen instead: isolate per file, matching the pattern this module already uses for `untracked` — `ingestOne` wraps each `ingestArtifact` call in try/catch, collecting `{path, error}` failures and continuing, so one bad file no longer silently aborts every file after it in the same batch. `retryDanglingRefs` (called at the end of every `kg:ingest` run, unconditionally) had the identical unguarded loop and got the same fix, though it was never named by this issue. Verified live: a batch of two files, one with a genuine full-slug-path collision, ingested the valid file and reported the broken one as a failure rather than throwing and aborting before the folder-tree rebuild.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Folder/Artifact-tier holders have no promotion channel at all, and nothing clears `.holder` once real content lands underneath**",
    "text": "**Folder/Artifact-tier holders have no promotion channel at all, and nothing clears `.holder` once real content lands underneath**: found live creating this very doc. `aperas resolve --create-holder` scaffolds a placeholder `ArtifactNode`/`Block` chain, and filling real content into the *Block* children works fine — but `kg:insert`'s promote path is Block-tier only (\"kg:insert only positions Block-level nodes\"), so there is no verb that ever flips a Folder/Artifact's own `.holder` back off, even once every child underneath it is real. `aperas project` correctly refuses to render such a subtree (\"still has a placeholder (holder) node somewhere in its subtree\"), but nothing resolves that refusal short of a direct edit or removing and recreating the artifact disk-first. Matches `Aperas-crud-design.md` §4.1/§6 exactly.. **Resolved, narrower than filed**: read directly, `ArtifactNode extends BlockNode` and already passes `kg:update`'s own type check — piping any content through `kg:update` on a holder `ArtifactNode` already cleared `.holder` before this fix, no code change needed there. The real gap was `FolderNode` alone: `kg:insert`'s move/promote mode was hard-restricted to `BlockNode`, and `kg:update` explicitly excludes `FolderNode` from its own target-kind check, so nothing graph-first ever reached `FolderNode.hydrateFromParsed`'s existing (and already-correct) `this.holder = undefined`. Fixed by extending `kg:insert`'s anchor-*less* bare-promote branch to also accept a `FolderNode` target — a `FolderNode` + `--after`/`--before` combination is rejected outright with a clear message, since a folder's position comes from its own path, never list order, so repositioning was never the missing piece. Verified live on a scratch holder chain (`resolve --create-holder`): the folder's `.holder` cleared on a bare promote, and the anchor combination was correctly rejected.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
