    ],
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1A7GR001:props:StringProp:00CFB1A9EG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1A7GR001:props:StringProp:00CFB1A9EG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CFB1A7GR001",
    "text": "**A parent-heading `update` is a third route to recreation, and neither the rule against it nor the promise that protects ids names it**: regrouping `planning/aperas-skill.md`'s flat 17-item Task Breakdown into three `###` batches was pushed as a single `aperas update` on the Task Breakdown heading, and came back `0 matched, 0 moved, 0 changed, 20 added, 17 removed` — every item tombstoned and reminted, because the items moved from direct children to grandchildren and the depth change defeated identity matching entirely. Recovered from the staged checkpoint (`git restore` of the working tree from the index, then `aperas reload -- --discard`), and redone the way the skill prescribes: batch headings created carrying placeholder anchors, each item repositioned with a stdin-less `insert` (cross-parent move, id preserved), placeholders tombstoned, then one small same-depth push per batch to set the `orderedList`/`startIndex` run-leader props — which only the very first item had ever carried, which is why batches 2 and 3 initially rendered as bullets. All 17 original ids verified live afterwards. Promoted to [issues/aperas-skill.md](../issues/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CFB1HX48001), with [the unconditional id-preservation promise](../issues/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CFB1HX48002) and [the unranked collision between the two sections](../issues/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CFB1HX48003) filed alongside it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8001",
      "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8002",
      "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8003",
      "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8004",
      "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8001:props:StringProp:00CFB1EAB0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8001:props:StringProp:00CFB1EAB0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CFB1E8X8001",
    "text": "**Why the rule didn't fire — it is keyed to a verb, not an outcome**: \"Move a node; don't remove and recreate it\" names `remove` + `insert` as the anti-pattern. This was an `update` on a parent heading; `remove` was never typed, and the tombstoning was a side effect of reconciliation rather than anything visible in the intent. A parent-heading push is a third route to recreation that the rule does not mention at all, so an operator following it faithfully can still destroy every id in a subtree.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CFB1A7GR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Why Mechanics pointed the other way — an unconditional promise**",
    "text": "**Why Mechanics pointed the other way — an unconditional promise**: \"Adding an item to an existing list\" prescribes exactly this call (\"target the list's parent heading… exact-key matching reuses every unchanged item's id\"), and for an ordered list calls it the only safe route. The id-preservation claim carries no precondition, though it only holds while a node keeps its parent and its depth. Two successful pushes in the same session (`17 matched`, and `11 matched, 6 added`) read as confirmation, so the generalisation from append to restructure felt earned rather than assumed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CFB1A7GR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The two sections collide, and nothing ranks them**",
    "text": "**The two sections collide, and nothing ranks them**: for \"relocate items inside an ordered list\", Discipline says move and Mechanics says parent-heading push. Mechanics is the more specific and more operational of the two, so it wins by default. The contradiction is not noted in either place.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CFB1A7GR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The through-line with the read-side failure**",
    "text": "**The through-line with the read-side failure**: both rules are keyed to the tool reached for rather than the outcome caused. Orientation keyed on \"editing the graph\" never fired for reading; identity-preservation keyed on `remove` never fired for `update`. The same restatement fixes both — name the outcome, then let it catch whatever verb produces it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CFB1A7GR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1E8X8005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The operator's own share, which is not the skill's fault**",
    "text": "**The operator's own share, which is not the skill's fault**: the risk was reasoned about explicitly (\"risk of mass tombstoning\") and the push went ahead anyway, because a check had shown zero backlinks on all 17 items and that made the downside feel cheap. That substitutes cost-of-being-wrong for what the rule actually says, and the rule also protects a node's place in history, which backlink count has no bearing on.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CFB1A7GR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1HX48001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1HX48001:props:StringProp:00CFB1HYK0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1HX48001:props:StringProp:00CFB1HYK8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CFB1HX48001",
    "text": "**The identity-preservation rule is keyed to a verb, so a parent-heading `update` slips past it**: \"Move a node; don't remove and recreate it\" names `remove` + `insert` as the anti-pattern, but `aperas update` on a parent heading recreates a whole subtree just as thoroughly, with the tombstoning arriving as a side effect of reconciliation rather than as anything the operator typed. Confirmed live: regrouping a flat 17-item Task Breakdown into three sub-headings came back `0 matched, 20 added, 17 removed`. The rule needs restating outcome-first — any operation that can tombstone a node meant to be kept — so it catches whatever verb produces that outcome instead of the one verb that was noticed first. **Resolved in v1.4**: the rule is now *Preserve identity — against any operation that can tombstone a node you meant to keep*, enumerating all three routes (`remove`+`insert`, a parent-heading `update`, and a depth-changing full-list push) rather than the one that looks like a delete, and adding that zero backlinks is not a licence.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1HX48002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CFB1HX48002",
    "text": "**Mechanics promises id preservation with no precondition, and the precondition is the whole story**: \"Adding an item to an existing list\" tells the reader to target the parent heading and states that exact-key matching reuses every unchanged item's id, calling it the only safe route for an ordered list. That promise holds only while a node keeps its parent and its depth; a push that moves items to a new depth silently matches nothing. Nothing in the section says so, so two successful appends read as licence to attempt a restructure the same way. **Resolved in v1.4**: the promise now carries its precondition — it holds only while every item keeps its parent and its depth — and says outright that stating it unconditionally is how it got read as licence for a restructure.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1HX48003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CFB1HX48003",
    "text": "**Discipline and Mechanics give opposite instructions for the same task, and nothing ranks them**: for relocating items inside an ordered list, Discipline says move the node while Mechanics says the parent-heading push is the only safe route. Mechanics is more specific and more operational, so it wins by default in exactly the case where it is wrong. Neither section acknowledges the other; whichever fix lands should leave a pointer in both directions rather than assuming a reader arrives at the right one. **Resolved in v1.4**: the two sections now point at each other, *Preserve identity* naming the matching promise it constrains and *Adding an item to an existing list* naming moves as the route for a depth change.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1T9BG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1T9BG008:links:Link:00CFB1TB48000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CFB1HX48001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CFB1T9BG008:links:Link:00CFB1TB48000:props:StringProp:00CFB1TB48001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "78"
          }
