  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGN8W9MG00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Tracked in the graph rather than accumulating here",
    "text": "Tracked in the graph rather than accumulating here: links silently failing to persist or resolve, confirmed twice in one session by unrelated mechanisms, with nothing detecting either automatically (`issues/linking.md`); `aperas resolve`'s title-ambiguity check not filtering tombstoned candidates, so a dead holder can still make a live path read as ambiguous (`discussion/core.md`'s Freeflow); a pre-existing, unreproduced `verify.ts` failure in the id-anchor emission idempotency check for list items/paragraphs (`planning/linking.md`'s Task Breakdown).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGN8W9MG009",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGNAAC3R002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CGNAAC3R002",
    "text": "`aperas tree`/`backlinks --text`/`unfold` all show a *rendered preview* — title plus truncated, anchor-stripped abstract. For a field they never show (`props`, `tombstonedAt`) or for a block's exact stored text, `aperas show <ref>` goes through the live service, so — unlike a raw-file reader — it always reflects the current in-memory state, not the last flush:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGN8W9MG000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGNAAC40000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CGNAAC40000",
    "text": "```bash\naperas show <ref>            # full record, exactly as stored: props, tombstonedAt, parent, children, links, title, text\naperas show <ref> --text     # exact stored text only, undecorated\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGN8W9MG000",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGNAAC40001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "`--text` is the one that matters before an edit",
    "text": "`--text` is the one that matters before an edit: redirect it to a file, change only what needs changing, and `cat` that back into `aperas update`. That keeps the untouched part of a block byte-identical instead of retyped from a preview — which is what step 2 of the edit loop warns about, since a transcription slip silently tombstones the block and mints a new id.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGN8W9MG000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGNAAC40002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CGNAAC40002",
    "text": "`scripts/show_node.py` still covers what `aperas show` doesn't — `--grep PATTERN` (full-text search; there is still no `aperas search`) and `--artifact <ref>` (which artifact a block lives in). It reads the **on-disk mirror**, so after an unflushed mutation it reports pre-flush state while `aperas show`/`unfold`/`tree` report the live service's. Flush first, or ask the CLI, when checking something you just changed. Caught live once, before `aperas show` existed: a move and a tombstone were both invisible to the script, producing a confident and wrong conclusion that the move had gone backwards.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGN8W9MG000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGQDBM58000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-15T13:12:25.772Z",
    "title": "**`flush --clobber` / `reload --discard` round-trip mechanics, startup GC, and shutdown logging design**",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGR1BM9G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**ApeironNgn service restart and `flush --clobber` silently dropped GC, startup skipped GC entirely, and every warning was unreachable regardless of wording**",
    "text": "**ApeironNgn service restart and `flush --clobber` silently dropped GC, startup skipped GC entirely, and every warning was unreachable regardless of wording**: `flush --clobber` performed mark-and-sweep tombstone GC (`pruneUnreachableTombstones`) without reporting what changed, service startup never ran the sweep at all (`service.ts:112` calls bare `rehydrateStore()`, contradicting `reloadStore`'s own doc comment claiming startup was already a sweep boundary), and `shutdown()`'s guarded `flushIfDirty()` could silently drop a computed GC result whenever disk had diverged externally. **First pass** (this same session) added a startup GC call and clearer wording on the shutdown warnings — reviewed afterward and found both incomplete: the startup GC computed but never set `dirty`/`stateDirty` (declared *after* that block ran), so it was never actually persisted — confirmed live via `flush --clobber` finding real, hours-old backlog the first time any non-`--discard` sweep boundary was hit at all; and every message involved, old or reworded, went to `console.error`, which is unreachable in real operation since the service is spawned with `stdio: 'ignore'` unconditionally (`serviceClient.ts`, both the dev and built-bundle spawn paths) — wording clarity was irrelevant when nothing could ever see it. Separately, `kg:reload`'s own GC counts were never surfaced at all, only `flush --clobber`'s. **Actually resolved**: reordered `service.ts` so `dirty`/`stateDirty` exist before the boot sweep runs, and the sweep now flushes immediately when it finds anything (safe at cold boot — disk can't have diverged yet, this store was just read from it); added a durable `logService()` helper (`service.ts`) that appends to a `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`-based log file alongside `console.error`, used for the boot-GC line, the shutdown warnings, and the timed-flush error paths; `reloadStore()` now returns the same GC metrics `clobberFlush()` already did, and `kg:reload` prints them the same way `kg:flush` does. The unrelated \"RDF deduplication\" this entry previously described as part of the same fix was a mischaracterization of a distinct, real bug — see the entry below.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGR1F620001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CGR1F620001:links:Link:00CHXY9140000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CHXSZ56R001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CGR1F620001:links:Link:00CHXY9140000:props:StringProp:00CHXY9140001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "639"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CGR1F620001:props:StringProp:00CGR1F62G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CGR1F620001:props:StringProp:00CGR1F62G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`flush --clobber` / `reload --discard` round-trip mechanics, startup GC, and shutdown logging design**",
    "text": "**`flush --clobber` / `reload --discard` round-trip mechanics, startup GC, and shutdown logging design**: investigated `reload --discard` and `flush --clobber` interaction, live, at the user's own request after a `flush --clobber` unexpectedly removed a real backlog. Mismatches observed were traced to two *unrelated* mechanisms conflated at first: mark-and-sweep tombstone GC (`pruneUnreachableTombstones`, legitimate — the backlog really was dead) and a duplicate-`@id` collision in `Profile.jsonld` (a real, separate data bug, wrongly described here as \"RDF deduplication\" as if it were a working, intentional mechanism — corrected in [issues/core.md](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CHXSZ56R001), which now carries its own entry). Discovered that prior service restarts left GC garbage on disk because `shutdown()`'s guarded `flushIfDirty()` silently refused and dropped a computed GC result whenever disk had diverged externally, while service startup never ran the sweep at all. First design pass: (1) run the sweep at startup, (2) reword the shutdown warnings, (3) return GC metrics from `flush --clobber`. Reviewed afterward, on request, for correctness and completeness against the actual code rather than the summary of what was intended — found both (1) and (2) didn't actually work: (1) computed the sweep but never set `dirty`/`stateDirty` (declared *after* that block ran in the original patch), so nothing was ever flushed; (2) and every other service-side message, including (1)'s own new log line, went to `console.error`, which is unreachable in real operation since the service is always spawned with `stdio: 'ignore'` (`serviceClient.ts`). Actually fixed: reordered the startup sequence so the sweep can flush immediately when it finds something, and added a durable `logService()` helper (append to a `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`-based log file) used everywhere `console.error` alone used to be the only channel. Also closed a gap the first pass didn't touch at all: `kg:reload` never surfaced its own GC counts, only `kg:flush --clobber` did — `reloadStore()` now returns the same shape and `kg:reload` prints it the same way.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CGRQKZQR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-16T03:41:22.024Z",
    "title": "### Standing link integrity checking script (`checkLinkIntegrity` / `aperas check-links`)",
    "text": "**Standing link integrity checking script (`checkLinkIntegrity` / `aperas check-links`)**: As specified in [issues/linking.md](../issues/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CGN6GJVG000), building a drift-style check script `checkLinkIntegrity` (and CLI verb `aperas check-links`) compares live block text against `.links` in Oxigraph Store. Scans for live nodes where internal link codes (`[[code]]`, `aperas://...`, `#id/BlockNode:...`) exist in `node.text` but have no corresponding resolved `Link` in `.links`. Supports `--repair` flag to auto-re-resolve and flush missing links, catching both historical drift and transient regressions without needing to know the root cause. Executed live sweep on corpus (`aperas check-links --repair`), detecting 40 historical link integrity discrepancies across 7 artifacts, re-resolving them all, and verifiably restoring the graph to 0 discrepancies.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGMX88R0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CH47P6K8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
