    "title": "### Mechanics — list manipulation section rewritten",
    "text": "Supersedes: [v2's own \"Adding an item to an existing list\" heading](#id/BlockNode:00CGF613WG005), [its depth caveat](#id/BlockNode:00CGF613WG006), [its ordered-list safe-route paragraph](#id/BlockNode:00CGF613WG008), [its unordered list instruction](#id/BlockNode:00CGF613WG009), [its input-rules paragraph](#id/BlockNode:00CGF613WG00A), and [v2.2's run-leader fix](#id/BlockNode:00CGN8W9MG008).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CH5FJMDR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Inserting an item into an existing list",
    "text": "For an **unordered** list, the safest and cleanest route is to insert the new item directly as a sibling of an existing one: `aperas insert <parent> --after <existing-item>`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CH5FJMDR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0002",
      "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0003",
      "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0004",
      "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0002:props:StringProp:00CH5FJQT8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0002:props:StringProp:00CH5FJQT8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The input rule**",
    "text": "**The input rule**: Pipe the bare item text **with its bullet marker** (e.g. `- new item`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Never pipe plain text** with no bullet",
    "text": "**Never pipe plain text** with no bullet: it parses as a `paragraph`, which breaks a contiguous list run in two.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CH5FJME0004",
    "text": "Should you meet an item nested inside a wrapper list, promote it out with `aperas insert <item-id> --after <existing-direct-child-of-the-list>` and `aperas remove` the emptied wrapper.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CH5FJME0005",
    "text": "For an **ordered** list, direct insertion is riskier because a freshly inserted item becomes its own run-leader and can restart the numbering rather than continuing it. (Though a recent fix clears this for the common case of landing next to a plain continuation item, landing next to a run-leader still renders a spurious blank line). \nThe safer route for ordered lists is to target the list's **parent heading** (`aperas update <heading-id>`) and pipe the heading line plus the *complete* list (every existing item verbatim plus the new one).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0006",
      "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0006:props:StringProp:00CH5FJQTG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0006:props:StringProp:00CH5FJQTG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CH5FJME0006",
    "text": "Exact-key matching reuses every unchanged item's id, **but only while every item keeps its parent and depth**. A push that changes item depth (e.g. regrouping under sub-headings) recreates everything; use `aperas insert` (moves) for that instead.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CH5FJME8000",
    "text": "**This only works if the piped content is genuinely complete.** Piping the heading plus *only* the new item reconciles the existing ones away as removed, tombstoning real content.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME0005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Updating an existing list item",
    "text": "To update the text of an *existing* list item without changing its identity, use `aperas update <item-id>`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CH5FJMDR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8002",
      "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8002:props:StringProp:00CH5FJQTR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8002:props:StringProp:00CH5FJQTR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The input rule**",
    "text": "**The input rule**: Pipe the bare text **without any bullet marker** (e.g. `updated text`, never `- updated text`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Explanation**",
    "text": "**Explanation**: The target node is already a `listItem`. If you pipe a `- `, the parser sees a *new list*, and `update` will replace the existing item's children with this new list, resulting in a nested list rendering bug (`- - updated text`). By piping bare text, it parses as a paragraph, and `update` correctly adopts its text into the existing list item.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CH5FJME8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CHXSWEZG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CHXSWEZG000",
    "text": "**A hand-edit collapsed two distinct `Profile` documents onto one `@id`, and `rehydrateStore`/`dehydrateToJsonLd` had no way to notice** — `Apeiron/Profile.jsonld` carried two separate entries (`handle: \"claude\"` and `handle: \"antigravity\"`) both under `Profile:00CDD5NTXG000` (introduced in the same commit that renamed two other profiles into these two, `403527b`). `rehydrateStore`'s `encodeDoc` keys every quad by `@id`, so both documents' fields land on one subject; `dehydrateToJsonLd` can then only ever emit one JSON document per subject, so one of the two silently disappears on every subsequent flush/reload/clobber — confirmed live, reproduced by forcing a `flush --clobber` and watching the \"claude\" entry vanish while \"antigravity\" survived. Previously described in this same entry (before this correction) as \"RDF deduplication,\" as if it were a working, intentional mechanism — it never was; there was no duplicate-`@id` detection anywhere. The `Profile.jsonld` collision itself was already fixed in a later commit (`antigravity` now has its own distinct id) before this was root-caused. **Resolved**: `rehydrateStore`/`encodeDoc` (`store.ts`) now collect every `@id` seen more than once into a new `RehydrateResult.duplicateIds` array — surfaced, not auto-resolved (picking a winner would just be a second, quieter way to lose the same data) — and both the initial boot rehydrate and `kg:reload` log a durable warning naming every colliding id whenever the array is non-empty.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CHXSZ56R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CHXSZ56R001",
    "text": "**A hand-edit collapsed two distinct `Profile` documents onto one `@id`, and `rehydrateStore`/`dehydrateToJsonLd` had no way to notice** — `Apeiron/Profile.jsonld` carried two separate entries (`handle: \"claude\"` and `handle: \"antigravity\"`) both under `Profile:00CDD5NTXG000` (introduced in the same commit that renamed two other profiles into these two, `403527b`). `rehydrateStore`'s `encodeDoc` keys every quad by `@id`, so both documents' fields land on one subject; `dehydrateToJsonLd` can then only ever emit one JSON document per subject, so one of the two silently disappears on every subsequent flush/reload/clobber — confirmed live, reproduced by forcing a `flush --clobber` and watching the \"claude\" entry vanish while \"antigravity\" survived. Previously described in this same entry (before this correction) as \"RDF deduplication,\" as if it were a working, intentional mechanism — it never was; there was no duplicate-`@id` detection anywhere. The `Profile.jsonld` collision itself was already fixed in a later commit (`antigravity` now has its own distinct id) before this was root-caused. **Resolved**: `rehydrateStore`/`encodeDoc` (`store.ts`) now collect every `@id` seen more than once into a new `RehydrateResult.duplicateIds` array — surfaced, not auto-resolved (picking a winner would just be a second, quieter way to lose the same data) — and both the initial boot rehydrate and `kg:reload` log a durable warning naming every colliding id whenever the array is non-empty.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
