  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ02QEF8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Mechanics — `aperas tree` names the node it started from, once — not every line",
    "text": "The first line of any `aperas tree` render is a breadcrumb, `aperas://tree/<path>` — the walkable path of the node the command was actually pointed at (`.` if none given), directly reusable as a `<ref>` elsewhere with no separate `aperas path` call. It appears exactly once, at the top: every line under it is already relative to that node by construction (that is what a tree render is), so repeating the prefix on each one would say nothing new. Omitted, not shown broken, when the node's own path can't be walked.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ02BTG0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CJ02VX60002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ02VX60002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CJ02VX60002",
    "text": "`--view` reuses the same idea at a second place it's actually needed: when a link escapes its owner's own viewcone into a nested one (Aperas-treeview-design.md §13 — the target is unfolded independently, and the link, not the target's own structural position, wins canonical placement), everything under that link is now relative to *it*, not to the render's original apex. That position gets its own one-time breadcrumb too, indented to match, for the same reason the apex gets one. An ordinary in-cone link (target already reachable from the apex the normal way) or a \"this is superseded, see elsewhere\" pointer line needs neither — nothing changes what \"here\" means at those positions.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ02QEF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0HX2Y0000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0HX2Y0000:props:StringProp:00CJ0HX7M8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0HX2Y0000:props:StringProp:00CJ0HX7M8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CJ0HX2Y0000",
    "text": "**Why isolation helps is still unexplained** — narrower and lower-stakes now that the split above removes the actual pain, but the mechanism itself is still just \"two ruled-out hypotheses and one unconfirmed one\" (see the entry above: not oxigraph's own memory footprint, not V8 heap-size — leading guess is inline-cache megamorphism or similar JIT-level degradation in a long process running many differently-shaped operations, never directly confirmed). Worth a closer look sometime with real V8 profiling (`--prof`/`--trace-deopt`) if it recurs somewhere a process split isn't a viable fix, but not worth chasing further here.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0JZ830001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T12:29:36.010Z",
    "title": "00CJ0JZ830001",
    "text": "**`aperas update --text-only` on an existing listItem can overwrite its title with an unrelated freshly-minted id** — found incidentally while editing this doc's own entries above, not investigated: a listItem's `title` normally falls back to its own bare id when its text has no colon lead-in (expected, confirmed on an untouched sibling), but after re-pushing text (also with no colon lead-in) via `update --text-only`, the title changed to a *different* id string that matches neither the target nor anything else nearby — looks like the non-heading title re-derivation added earlier this session (`kgUpdate.ts`, [issues/core.md above](#id/BlockNode:00CGM54FX0001)) picks up a throwaway id minted for the freshly re-parsed input tree, rather than leaving the title alone when there's no real lead-in to derive. Cosmetic today (nothing addresses this node by title-derived path), not reproduced under controlled conditions, not fixed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ0QG048001",
