    ],
    "title": "**The webapp is the UI-projection tier, so it never owns fold state**",
    "text": "**The webapp is the UI-projection tier, so it never owns fold state**: [three tiers of perata](../design/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW4Y8009) names a Human UI as a consumer of an i-projection, \"rather than reading a raw fold-state flag off the graph directly\". Every fold control is therefore a *controlled* component whose state is `TreeView.unfolds` membership, round-tripped through `unfold`/`fold` — there is no local expanded/collapsed state anywhere in the UI.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Deep read, shallow write**",
    "text": "**Deep read, shallow write**: the UI reads by traversal as freely as the CLI does, but writes only a block's own body text. Any block's body, not only leaves — headings' titles and every structural operation (insert, move, remove, relink) are delegated to the Agent.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Why that write boundary is principled, not just an MVP scope cut**",
    "text": "**Why that write boundary is principled, not just an MVP scope cut**: body-text replacement is the one edit class that cannot tombstone anything. A parent-heading `update` reconciles and remints its subtree as a side effect; a push that changes item depth defeats matching entirely; `remove` + `insert` destroys ids outright. Confining the UI to text keeps every id, backlink and history position safe by construction rather than by care.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR004:links:Link:00CJ2W4WBR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CE1GW4YG000",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR004:links:Link:00CJ2W4WBR000:props:StringProp:00CJ2W4WBR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "313"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`FolderDiv`'s three slots are already the three render tiers**",
    "text": "**`FolderDiv`'s three slots are already the three render tiers**: `heading` is tier 1 (title only), `unfoldable` is tier 2 (`{title, abstract}`), `foldable` is tier 3 (children and links). This is why the port is a natural fit rather than a coincidence, and it is why the component needs no new concepts to carry [a node's own-line detail tiers](../design/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW4YG000).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**What the port drops**",
    "text": "**What the port drops**: the `slotchange` handler that sniffed for a leading `<h*>` element existed because HTML carried no type information; `BlockNode.type` already answers it, so the Solid component takes an explicit `variant` of `heading` / `item` / `leaf` instead. Shadow DOM goes; the CSS custom properties it exposed through `:host` (`--control-foreground`, `--stem-line-foreground`, `--contents-indent`, and the rest) are kept verbatim as the theming surface, as is the hover-highlighted stem line.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR006:links:Link:00CJ2W4WBG001",
