            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "170"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Links render at two sites sharing one identity**",
    "text": "**Links render at two sites sharing one identity**: the link's own text inside the block's prose, and an endnote entry in the link list after the children — the position [`aperas tree --view`](../design/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW4YG004) already puts it in. Wikipedia's `cite-ref`/`cite-note` scheme, minus the superscript marker: Wikipedia needs `[1]` because a citation has no visible text of its own, whereas a wikilink already carries the words the reader is looking at, so the link text *is* the marker — it holds `cite-ref-<linkId>-<n>`, jumps to the endnote on click, and is what the endnote's back-caret returns to. What a marker would otherwise have signalled — that an endnote exists below — is carried by styling instead: a wikilink that resolved to a graph node is styled as one, a dangling one is not, which is a distinction worth showing in its own right.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Hovering a link previews, and can promote**",
    "text": "**Hovering a link previews, and can promote**: the popover shows the rule-a preview and offers an unfold control that adds the `Link` itself to `unfolds` — which is what makes the endnote below expand. One source of truth; the popover has no fold state of its own. The hover target is the link text itself rather than a separate glyph, so the reader hovers the words already being read.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR008:links:Link:00CJ2W4WB8001",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CE1GW4YG006",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR008:links:Link:00CJ2W4WB8001:props:StringProp:00CJ6906CR000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "226"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Both sites obey canonical position rather than re-deriving it**",
    "text": "**Both sites obey canonical position rather than re-deriving it**: how a link's target renders is not a property of the link — it is a property of where that target's structural home sits relative to the current render, which [canonical position](../design/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW4YG006) already decides. Three states. The home is nowhere in the render, and the link renders the target itself — a rule-a preview folded, rule-b one level unfolded. The home is in the render, even as a bare title-only breadcrumb, and the link collapses to the `[*see <path>]` pointer plus a jump, never a second copy of the body. The target sits above the apex, and it renders as `(outside view)` plus a zoom-out. Obeying rather than re-deriving is aimed at a specific temptation: the popover and the endnote are two code paths, and each will locally find it easy to just show the target, at which point the two can disagree with each other and with `aperas tree` while nothing looks broken. Both must read one shared verdict. It also makes link-following cycle-safe for free, so the UI needs no visited set of its own.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR009:links:Link:00CJ2W4WB0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CE1GW4YG008",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR009:links:Link:00CJ2W4WB0000:props:StringProp:00CJ2W4WB8000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "126"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Zoom in is a new apex, zoom out is the breadcrumb**",
    "text": "**Zoom in is a new apex, zoom out is the breadcrumb**: ctrl-clicking a fold arrow re-renders with that node as apex, which is [viewcone zoom](../design/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW4YG008) with no new mechanism; the breadcrumb bar is the apex's own ancestor chain, and clicking a segment re-apexes there. A nested cone gets its own inline breadcrumb strip, for the same reason the text render emits one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V1YAR00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The apex is UI-local, not view state**",
    "text": "**The apex is UI-local, not view state**: `TreeView` deliberately holds `unfolds` and not an apex, and this design does not add one. Zoom history is browser state; a reload returns to the root.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V5K2G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V5K2G001:props:StringProp:00CJ2V5ND8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V5K2G001:props:StringProp:00CJ2V5ND8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Transport: an HTTP listener inside the ApeironNgn service**",
    "text": "**Transport: an HTTP listener inside the ApeironNgn service**: the UI has to show the Agent's deep writes as they land, and live notification can only honestly originate in the service — it is the sole writer and the only thing that knows when the store changed, so a separate bridge cannot push what the protocol never tells it. That decides it against the bridge, and a Node-hosted shell is out on delivery cost and on the two-writer hazard besides. The unix socket stays exactly as it is for CLI callers; the listener is an addition beside it, not a replacement for it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V5K2R000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Change notification is a bare tick, not a delta**",
    "text": "**Change notification is a bare tick, not a delta**: the service pushes *the store changed* and the UI refetches its current apex. The UI renders a view's `unfolds` set rather than a document with cursor state, so there is nothing a per-node delta would preserve that a refetch loses, and no delta format has to be invented to find that out. Carried over SSE — one-directional, no framing library, trivially proxied — which is a second protocol shape alongside today's one-request-per-connection NDJSON rather than a reuse of it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V5K2R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Deep-write handoff is Phase 2+**",
    "text": "**Deep-write handoff is Phase 2+**: composing an intent and handing it to the Agent — a copied prompt, an MCP endpoint, an embedded chat pane — is out of the first phase entirely, unexamined rather than decided against. That pins Phase 1's whole write surface to one thing: an in-place edit of the current node's own text, which is an ordinary `update --text-only` over the same channel.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V5K2R002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**One link cited twice in the same block is already the stored shape**",
    "text": "**One link cited twice in the same block is already the stored shape**: resolution groups a block's wikilinks by distinct *target* (`positionsByTarget`, in `apeironNgn/artifacts.ts`), so one `Link` carries one `position` prop per occurrence, and identity is the target alone — position is metadata on that identity, which is why two occurrences that merely swap order still count as a real change. Wikipedia's `^ a b` is therefore nothing to add: one endnote per `Link`, one back-reference per `position`, and those positions are exactly the link-text occurrences that serve as the markers.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2V5K2R003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The UI's text input is the ingestion input**",
    "text": "**The UI's text input is the ingestion input**: no projection anchor has to be preserved across an in-place edit, because none of them are stored. A heading's tree-anchors are scooped into the `treeAnchor` prop and stripped from its title at parse; the `aperas-id` anchor is generated fresh at projection time and never written back; and `stripInlineAnchors` runs at the point of storage itself, so an anchor cannot enter a block's stored text through ingestion or through `update`/`insert` either. The editor therefore round-trips plain authored markdown — the same text the `.md` file carried before projection spliced anything into it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2VBQR0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2VBQR0001:props:StringProp:00CJ2VBT28000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2VBQR0001:props:StringProp:00CJ2VBT28001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`packages/web`'s code inventory, 2026-09-17**",
    "text": "**`packages/web`'s code inventory, 2026-09-17**: the unmodified Vite+Solid scaffold — `App.tsx`, `index.tsx`, two CSS files, nothing else. It declares `@aperas/core` as a dependency and imports it nowhere, which is just as well: 14 files under `packages/core/src` import `node:fs`, `node:path` or `oxigraph`, so core cannot be bundled for a browser at all. That dependency is a scaffold leftover and should move to whatever host actually runs the engine.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2VBQR0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The renderer already splits plan from text**",
    "text": "**The renderer already splits plan from text**: `renderTreeWithView` in `packages/core/src/apeironNgn/node.ts` runs two passes: `discoverCone` builds a render *plan* (`ConeInfo` — which ids are unfolded in this cone, which links escape it, which nested cone each escaping link spawned, the shared canonical/attempt maps) and only then does `emitNode`/`emitLinkLine` flatten it to `string[]`. Pass 1 is exactly what a UI wants; pass 2 is the only part that assumes text.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2VBQR0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Proposed shape of the structured render**",
    "text": "**Proposed shape of the structured render**: A `buildRenderTree(...)` returning a tree of render items — for a node: id, display label, title, abstract, tier, hidden count, and the starred / tombstoned / holder flags; for a link: link id, predicate, target id, title, abstract, a mode of `preview` / `expanded` / `pointer` / `outside-view`, the pointer target or zoom path where those apply, and its own children and links. Today's text output becomes `toText(buildRenderTree(...))`, which keeps the existing verification suite as the guarantee that the refactor changed nothing — the text render must stay byte-identical. A `format` discriminator on the existing `tree` service op carries it to a caller.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2VBQR0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The transport comparison in full**",
    "text": "**The transport comparison in full**: *A separate bridge*: the service changes not at all — NDJSON over a unix socket, single-writer discipline, lock file, code-fingerprint check and conflict reporting all untouched — and the bridge can reuse `serviceClient`'s own spawn helpers, keep the socket filesystem-scoped, and host web-only concerns that should never enter the engine protocol. Against it: a third process alongside the service and the dev server, one connection per request per hop given the protocol is one-request-per-connection, a duplicated slice of error handling, and no answer yet for who starts it in a packaged build. *An in-service listener*: one process, one lifecycle, one hop, conflict and fingerprint reports reaching the browser verbatim from where they are produced, and the only honest origin for change notification. Against it: the service today binds a unix socket with filesystem permissions and no auth whatsoever, so a TCP listener needs origin checking and probably a token before it is safe against any page in the browser — a design task, not a flag — and it further entrenches `packages/cli` owning the service. *A Node-hosted shell*: no wire at all, deep read as a function call, and `@aperas/core` in the UI's dependencies finally honest. Against it: much the largest delivery story, against a repo whose packaging concern is still open; it forecloses opening the UI in a browser; Tauri reintroduces a bridge under another name while Electron ships Chromium; and a shell embedding core in-process while a CLI service also runs gives two writers to one mirror, which is the single thing the service exists to prevent — unless the shell simply is the service, which is the in-service listener again.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ2VBQR0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Write-path footguns the UI must hide**",
    "text": "**Write-path footguns the UI must hide**: The input rules are per node type and unforgiving: an existing list item takes bare text with no bullet marker, or `update` replaces its children with a nested list; a heading needs `--text-only`, or reconciliation tombstones its subtree from an empty body. A single `updateText(id, text)` on the host side should pick the right form per type so the UI never encodes any of it. The per-artifact link sweep the service already runs after every write, and the known failure where a write reports links resolved but persists none, both have to surface in the UI rather than being swallowed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
