    ],
    "title": "00CJ5FATY8003",
    "text": "Graph-tree merge mechanics (what \"a member graph merges into its team graph\" actually does at the node/id level), and how upward `aperas.config.json` discovery behaves when none exists at all. See [Settled: graph discovery](#id/BlockNode:00CDHSCK8R001) below.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ5ET5Z8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ69EA7G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The canonical-position verdict moves into `@aperas/core`**",
    "text": "**The canonical-position verdict moves into `@aperas/core`**: that computation lives inside the CLI's tree renderer today, so obeying it from the webapp means extracting it as a pure function over a view's `unfolds` set, the current apex and a node, returning the render verdict — with the CLI renderer becoming its first consumer rather than its owner. Both of the webapp's two link sites then read the same function the CLI reads, which is what makes \"obey rather than re-derive\" enforceable instead of merely intended.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ69GNM0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ69GNM0001:props:StringProp:00CJ69GQZR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ69GNM0001:props:StringProp:00CJ69GQZR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Auth for the in-service listener**",
    "text": "**Auth for the in-service listener**: the threat is the browser, not the network — once the service listens on TCP, any page the user has open can reach it, and CORS does not stop a simple cross-origin POST from being *sent*, only from being read. Five controls, all cheap. The listener serves an explicit op allowlist — deep read plus `update --text-only` on one node — rather than proxying the whole service protocol; this bounds the damage of every other failure and is the one decision that is hard to retrofit. Every request carries a custom `X-Aperas-Token` header, which forces a CORS preflight and so stops a cross-origin request from ever being sent, independent of the token's value. The listener binds `127.0.0.1` explicitly, never `0.0.0.0`. The token is a per-run `randomBytes(32)` written into the existing run directory beside the lock and socket at mode 0600 — already the trust anchor for the socket, so no new secret-storage story — read by the dev server, which runs as the same user, and compared with `timingSafeEqual`. `Host` is rejected unless it is the loopback address, which is the specific anti-DNS-rebinding control, and `Origin` is allowlisted as defence in depth, never reflected back. TLS on loopback, accounts and request signing buy nothing against this threat model.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ2TESY0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ69H0T0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ69H0T0001:props:StringProp:00CJ69H2W0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
