        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**2026-09-18: legacy `aperas-id` anchors swept from stored text**",
    "text": "**2026-09-18: legacy `aperas-id` anchors swept from stored text**: 49 live blocks still carried an inline `<a name='id/...' class='aperas-anchor aperas-id'></a>` tag inside their own stored `text` — a remnant of the earlier convention that kept the anchor as literal content, left behind when that switched to scooping a heading's anchor into the `treeAnchor` prop and stripping a list item/paragraph's at the point of storage (`astParser.ts`'s `stripInlineAnchors` call in `convertAstNode`; see [Anchors](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4T800H)). Every affected block predates that fix and none created after it were affected, confirming the write path has been correct since. Swept by piping each block's own stored text back through `aperas update --text-only`, so the strip was performed by the engine's own function rather than by hand, then verified byte-for-byte against independently computed expected output: 0 unquoted anchors left corpus-wide, with no change to any block's type, parent, children or link count. 35 of the tags named their own block's id and were invisible in projection, since `project.ts`'s `hasIdAnchor` idempotency check suppressed the fresh splice; 14 named *another* block's id — the fingerprint of content copied out of an already-projected file — and rendered as a visibly doubled anchor. All were inert for resolution, because `resolveFragmentCode` routes an `id/` fragment straight to the direct-id tier without ever consulting `candidateCarriesAnchor`, but not harmless: five blocks in `discussion/aperas-skill.md` had been falling back to a bare-id title because the tag sat between their lead-in colon and the rest, blocking lead-in derivation. All five derive correctly now.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBZ7698003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
